{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"🚨 **IMPORTANT: boot wait left at the 90s default — the suite convention for this exact flake class is 300s+**

This PR's own root-cause comment names the JIT boot as the culprit, but the new `waitForBoot()` call keeps the 90s default. Other tests that were hardened against the same "loaded runner" failure class raised the boot window explicitly, with a documented rationale:

```dart
// test/integration/agents_hub_overlay_pty_test.dart:44
// Generous: a loaded box (sibling test loops) can take minutes to
// jit the entrypoint; the 90s default misreads that as a dead boot.
await harness.waitForBoot(timeout: const Duration(seconds: 300));
```

The same 300s (or 5m in `job_card_heredoc_pty_test.dart`) is used in `providers_queue_pty_test.dart:61`, `steering_persistence_pty_test.dart:74`, and `termios_xoff_pty_test.dart:96`. On a box where JIT boot takes minutes, this test will re-flake at boot with the same `TimeoutException` class gh-1012 quarantined — and 6/6 *local* green runs cannot exercise that regime, because a local box is never that loaded.

Suggested fix:

```dart
await harness.waitForBoot(timeout: const Duration(seconds: 300));
```

Note: this pairs with the per-test `@Timeout(Duration(minutes: 5))` cap — see the other comment before applying, since a 300s boot window exceeds that cap on its own.
